{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1518452681,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUyOTY0MzY3MTY=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/2147","origin":"github"},"title":"open_r_encdetect -- 1kB might not be enough!","message":"#### What is the problem?\n`open_r_encdetect` helper promises to guess the encoding of the file by first reading first 1kB and guessing the type from it, and then reading using `io.open` while providing encoding\n\nIMHO it is principally incorrect -- unicode character might happen to come anywhere in the text file (happens for 53) even in the last position in the text.  \n\noverall `io` module afaik is more of a plumbing than user-oriented.  I do not see why we should not just read the entire file in bytes, and then guess encoding on the entirety of the content.  I thought even to some degree that is what stock `open` is intended to do and does in this particular use case just fine:\n```\n*(Pdb) print type(open(README_fname).read())\n\u003ctype 'unicode'\u003e\n```\nso what problem this helper was intended to solve?","files":null}]}